      "textEn": "107\n\nAnd Jayanti, while following the rituals prescribed by the Shruti and Smriti scriptures, presented the ideal of household life. Meaning: By being the incarnation of the complete Purushottam Bhagwan, Rishabhdev had no\nresponsibility from worldly affairs. As it is said in the Bhagavad Gita—Paritranaya sadhunam vinashaya cha dushkritam—\nThe purpose of incarnation is to protect the devotees and to stop the demonic actions of the non-devotees. To take incarnation of God,\nthese are the only two purposes. Shri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu has said that before giving advice, a person should\nthat he should lead a practical life and show people how to do the work. Yours\nAchari' devotion, all the peaks are covered—until someone acts in the same way, until then, he cannot teach others. Rishabhdev was an ideal king, and he received education in Gurukul, although he\nwas already educated because God is omniscient.\nAlthough there was nothing in the Gurukul that was worthy of their learning,\nthey went there to teach people how\neducation is obtained from Vedic gurus. After this, he entered the household life and started living life according to the Vedic method in accordance with the Shruti-Smriti. Srila Rupa Goswami, in Bhakti Rasamruta Sindhu (1.2.10), while presenting the following quote from Skanda Purana, says—\n\nWithout the Vedas, Smritis, Puranas, and other five types of Vidhis. The solitude is born of the devotion to the Lord. Human society should follow the teachings received from Vedic literature such as Shruti and Smriti. According to the Pancha-Ratrika method, this is the worship of God in practical life. Every person should\nwant to live a divine life and ultimately go to the abode of God. Maharaj Rishabhdev strictly followed all these\nrules. They remained ideal householders and they taught their sons\nto achieve perfection in spiritual life.\nThe way he ruled the earth,\nand fulfilled his purpose as an incarnation, these are some examples of t