      "textEn": "108\n\nMeaning of the word\n\nYesham—from whom; Khalu—certainly; Maha-yogi—a great devotee of God; Bharata—Bharat; Jyeshtha—the eldest; Shreshtha-gunah—\nEndowed with excellent qualities; Aasit—was; Yena—by which; Idam—this; Varsham—people, country; Bharatam—India; Iti—Thus;\nVyapadishanti—People say. Among Rishabhdev's hundred sons, the eldest son's name was Bharat, who was a great devotee endowed with excellent qualities. In his honor, this world is called Bharatavarsha. Meaning: This land, called Bharatavarsha, is also called a sacred land. In today's time, the land of India or\nBharatvarsh is a small piece of land stretching from the Himalayan mountains to the Kumari Antarip. Sometimes,\nthis peninsula is called the sacred land. Shri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu has given special\nimportance to the inhabitants of this land (Chaitanyacharitamrita, Adi 9.41)—\n\nIn the land of India, human birth is a friend. Birth meaningful by doing good to others'. “Whoever has taken birth in India in human form should make their life meaningful through benevolence.\nShould make it.\n\" The inhabitants of this land are extremely fortunate. They can purify their birth by accepting the Krishna consciousness movement,\nand by going outside of India, they can preach it for the welfare of the entire world. Tamanu Kushavarta Ilavarto Brahmavarto Malayah Ketur Bhadrasen Indrasprugvidarbhah Kikat Iti Nav Navati.\nPradhana:. 10>. Meaning of the word\n\nTam—That; Anu—following; Kushavarta—Kushavarta; Ilavarta—Ilavarta; Brahmavarta—Brahmavarta; Malayah—Malay;\nKetu—Ketu; Bhadra-senah—Bhadrasenah; Indra-spruk—Indraspruk; Vidarbha—Vidarbha; Kikatah—Kikat; Iti—Thus; Nav—Nine;\nNavati—Ninety; Pradhayah—Bigger than. Besides Bharat, they also had ninety-nine other sons. Among these, the names of the nine elder sons were Kushavarta,\n\nIlavarta, Brahmavarta, Malaya, Ketu, Bhadrasen, Indrasprk, Vidarbha, and Kikat. Kavirahavirantarikashcha Prabuddhah Pippalayanah. Aavihrotrodha, drum, chakra, lotus. 11."
